<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Missi dominici</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The missi dominici, 'envoys of the lord', were officials sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> to supervise local administration across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>. Usually travelling in pairs of a churchman and a layman, they checked on counts, heard complaints and announced royal decrees. They were a key means of maintaining central control.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The office grew out of Merovingian ad hoc arrangements, under which a missus regis, the king's envoy, was sent out as needed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, who ruled from 768 to 814, made the envoys a regular part of his government. At first he chose them from his most trusted entourage, whatever their social rank. The Lorsch Annals record that in 802 he turned instead to archbishops, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots, dukes and counts, men who had no need to take gifts from the people they judged, and the Capitulare missorum generale of that year set out their duties in detail.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Their circuits, called missatica or legationes, were drawn so as not to follow the lines of the existing dioceses or provinces, and the missi were to visit them for a month at a time, four times a year. They were chosen afresh each year and were usually strangers to their district, so that they would not put down local roots or act on their own initiative, as the counts were doing. Envoys sent for a single purpose were called missi ad hoc, and extraordinary missi represented the emperor on special occasions, sometimes beyond his borders.</p><p>Besides justice, they received oaths of allegiance to the king, made his will known, at times by distributing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitularies</a>, and supervised the clergy of their region. The inhabitants had to provide for their keep, and at times the missi led the host to battle. They were protected by a triple wergeld, and resisting them was punishable by death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Even under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> it was hard to find men who would carry out these duties impartially, and after his death it became almost impossible. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> the nobles came to influence the choice of missi, who were then taken from the districts they served; the list of 825 shows circuits that matched the provinces. Their work merged with that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and counts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> is not known to have sent any out. The missi disappeared from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> around the end of the 9th century and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> during the 10th, as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> took the place of central control.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Missus dominicus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This was enough to sustain the court and the <b>missi dominici</b>, but not enough to maintain an army independent of aristocratic military service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne and Louis the Pious ruled through assemblies, written ordinances called capitularies, travelling royal agents called <b>missi dominici</b> and the network of counts, and tried to bind the realm together through a common Christian discipline, set out in the Admonitio generalis of 789.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
